import "core"
import "core"


# A list literal of two or more elements written into a field of a data type.
  The companion to @listField.ct@, which checks the same field holding nothing and holding one
  thing: the second cell is what this adds, and for a long time it was what a list field could not
  reach. Written for two of the SAME string as well as two different ones, because the two are not
  the same check — a spine reads its element type from every cell it holds, and two of one string
  would pass on a rule that only looked at the first.
  What it took to hold: the [tail] a [/Data/List/Cons] carries is spelled @∀ConsList[/T=$^/T]@, a
  parent reference naming the cons's own [/T] from a nested position. Reading that occurrence as a
  membership position — the tail's elements are members of [/T] exactly as the [head] is — is what
  lets the spine take its element type from the whole of itself rather than from its head, so the
  [/T] of the inner cons is the [/Data/Primitive/String] the field was declared to hold rather than
  the outer head's literal.
  These values are declared rather than printed, so what this holds is the typechecking of a second
  cell. Printing a list field is held beside it: @listFieldNested.ct@ prints elements which are data
  arms, and @valueDeclFields.ct@ prints a one-element field on a type carrying three field shapes.
data(Tagged(name -> String, tags -> /Data/List[$T -> String]))

every(Tagged.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

/Tagged(name= "two of the same", tags= ["x", "x"])
/Tagged(name= "two different", tags= ["x", "y"])

main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
